41 class Bash(ToolBase):
42     """The bash tool."""
43 
44     name: str = "Bash"
45     """The tool name presented to the agent."""
46 
47     description: str = """Executes a bash command and returns its output.
48 
49 The working directory persists between commands, but shell state does
50 not. The shell environment is initialized from the user's profile
51 (bash or zsh).
52 
53 IMPORTANT: Avoid using this tool to run `find`, `grep`, `cat`, `head`,
54 `tail`, `sed`, `awk`, or `echo` commands, unless explicitly instructed
55 or after you have verified that a dedicated tool cannot accomplish your
56 task. Instead, use the appropriate dedicated tool as this will provide
57 a much better experience for the user:
58 
59  - File search: Use Glob (NOT find or ls)
60  - Content search: Use Grep (NOT grep or rg)
61  - Read files: Use Read (NOT cat/head/tail)
62  - Edit files: Use Edit (NOT sed/awk)
63  - Write files: Use Write (NOT echo >/cat <<EOF)
64  - Communication: Output text directly (NOT echo/printf)
65 
66 While the Bash tool can do similar things, it's better to use the
67 built-in tools as they provide a better user experience and make it
68 easier to review tool calls and give permission.
69 
70 # Instructions
71  - If your command will create new directories or files, first use
72    this tool to run `ls` to verify the parent directory exists and is
73    the correct location.
74  - Always quote file paths that contain spaces with double quotes in
75    your command (e.g., cd "path with spaces/file.txt")
76  - Try to maintain your current working directory throughout the
77    session by using absolute paths and avoiding usage of `cd`. You may
78    use `cd` if the User explicitly requests it.
79  - You may specify an optional timeout in milliseconds (up to 600000ms
80    / 10 minutes). By default, your command will timeout after 120000ms
81    (2 minutes).
82  - Write a clear, concise description of what your command does. For
83    simple commands, keep it brief (5-10 words). For complex commands
84    (piped commands, obscure flags, or anything hard to understand at a
85    glance), include enough context so that the user can understand what
86    your command will do.
87  - When issuing multiple commands:
88   - If the commands are independent and can run in parallel, make
89     multiple Bash tool calls in a single message. Example: if you need
90     to run "git status" and "git diff", send a single message with two
91     Bash tool calls in parallel.
92   - If the commands depend on each other and must run sequentially,
93     use a single Bash call with '&&' to chain them together.
94   - Use ';' only when you need to run commands sequentially but don't
95     care if earlier commands fail.
96   - DO NOT use newlines to separate commands (newlines are ok in
97     quoted strings).
98  - For git commands:
99   - Prefer to create a new commit rather than amending an existing
100     commit.
101   - Before running destructive operations (e.g., git reset --hard, git
102     push --force, git checkout --), consider whether there is a safer
103     alternative that achieves the same goal. Only use destructive
104     operations when they are truly the best approach.
105   - Never skip hooks (--no-verify) or bypass signing (--no-gpg-sign,
106     -c commit.gpgsign=false) unless the user has explicitly asked for
107     it. If a hook fails, investigate and fix the underlying issue.
108  - Avoid unnecessary `sleep` commands:
109   - Do not sleep between commands that can run immediately — just run
110     them.
111   - Do not retry failing commands in a sleep loop — diagnose the root
112     cause or consider an alternative approach.
113   - If you must sleep, keep the duration short (1-5 seconds) to avoid
114     blocking the user."""
